      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "b0A",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 1086315643,
      "version": 94,
      "versionNonce": 259641715,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1792767097928,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "NOTES",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "center",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "NOTES",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
    {
      "id": "aWqsnfp4oqtd1QdbRhz7p",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 205.3502197265625,
      "y": 545.341357,
      "width": 639.299560546875,
      "height": 850,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "#b2f2bb",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "b0B",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 1432513237,
      "version": 1621,
      "versionNonce": 1707420435,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1792767097928,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "A barrier is a point in the program where every thread in\nthe block stops until the last one arrives; then all are\nreleased tohether. Use: write shared memory -> barrier ->\nread what others wrote.\n\nIt is a technique, not a default. A kernel without one has \nthreads that never look at each other.\n\nScope = one block. Threads in different blocks never share\na barrier; the only grid-wide \"barrier\" is the end of the kernel.\n\nEvery thread in the block must reach the same barrier call,\nthe same number of times. Two barriers in two branches\nof an if-else that splits the block = each group waits\nfor threads that are stuck at the other barrier =\ndeadlock (or silently wrong results). Same trap:\nan early `return` or a per-thread loop count that skips a barrier.\n\nThe hardware's side: a block starts only when one SM has room\nfor all its threads at once, so anyone at a barrier knows\nthe rest are running nearby and will arrive. This prevents\nresource deadlock; it does not fix the if-else bug, which\nis ours to avoid.\n\nBecause barriers stop at the block, blocks are independent,\nalways, with our without barriers. The hardware\nruns them in any order, 2 at a time or 400, and\nthe result is identical. That is transparent\nscalability.\n\nPrice: nothing inside a kernal can wait for all blocks.\nTo combine results across blocks, end the kernel\nand continue on the host or in a second\nlaunch.",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "center",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "A barrier is a point in the program where every thread in\nthe block stops until the last one arrives; then all are\nreleased tohether. Use: write shared memory -> barrier ->\nread what others wrote.\n\nIt is a technique, not a default. A kernel without one has \nthreads that never look at each other.\n\nScope = one block. Threads in different blocks never share\na barrier; the only grid-wide \"barrier\" is the end of the kernel.\n\nEvery thread in the block must reach the same barrier call,\nthe same number of times. Two barriers in two branches\nof an if-else that splits the block = each group waits\nfor threads that are stuck at the other barrier =\ndeadlock (or silently wrong results). Same trap:\nan early `return` or a per-thread loop count that skips a barrier.\n\nThe hardware's side: a block starts only when one SM has room\nfor all its threads at once, so anyone at a barrier knows\nthe rest are running nearby and will arrive. This prevents\nresource deadlock; it does not fix the if-else bug, which\nis ours to avoid.\n\nBecause barriers stop at the block, blocks are independent,\nalways, with our without barriers. The hardware\nruns them in any order, 2 at a time or 400, and\nthe result is identical. That is transparent\nscalability.\n\nPrice: nothing inside a kernal can wait for all blocks.\nTo combine results across blocks, end the kernel\nand continue on the host or in a second\nlaunch.",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
    {
      "id": "Gs5B7RjAbrvl0Z87g-xN9",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 1124.1899949387475,
      "y": -322.12176114285717,
      "width": 690.2796020507812,
      "height": 775,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#e03131",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "b0C",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 1834412509,
      "version": 1523,
      "versionNonce": 994094707,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1792767396431,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "One kernel launch produces a grid of 8 blocks. The\nsame grid is handed to 2 different GPUs. The small one\nhas room for 2 blocks at a time, so it works through\nthem in 4 rounds; the big one has room for 4, so it\nneeds 2 rounds and finishes in half the time. Neither\nGPU was told anything special: the program is identical,\n the blocks are identical, and the output is identical.\nOnly the speed changes.\n\nThis works because blocks never wait for each other \n(barriers stop at the block), so the hardware is\nfree to run them in any grouping or any order.\nThe programmer writes once; the GPU,\nwhatever size, decides how many to run at a time.\n\nBlocks are independent: nothing a block does\ndepends on when another block runs, because\nthe only waiting allowed (a barrier) stays inside a block. \nSo any order and any grouping gives the same result.\n\nEvery CUDA GPU honors the same rules for a block: all\nits threads on one SM, together, with the same\nthread indices and the same barrier behavior. So a block\nmeans the same on a small GPU and big one.\n\nSince blocks are independent, it lets the hardware shuffle\nblocks freely. Since every CUDA GPU honors the same rules for blocks,\nmeans shuffling never changes what a block computes.\n\nPut together: same program, any GPU, same answer. The GPU\nonly decides how many blocks to run at once, never what they do.",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "center",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "One kernel launch produces a grid of 8 blocks. The\nsame grid is handed to 2 different GPUs. The small one\nhas room for 2 blocks at a time, so it works through\nthem in 4 rounds; the big one has room for 4, so it\nneeds 2 rounds and finishes in half the time. Neither\nGPU was told anything special: the program is identical,\n the blocks are identical, and the output is identical.\nOnly the speed changes.\n\nThis works because blocks never wait for each other \n(barriers stop at the block), so the hardware is\nfree to run them in any grouping or any order.\nThe programmer writes once; the GPU,\nwhatever size, decides how many to run at a time.\n\nBlocks are independent: nothing a block does\ndepends on when another block runs, because\nthe only waiting allowed (a barrier) stays inside a block. \nSo any order and any grouping gives the same result.\n\nEvery CUDA GPU honors the same rules for a block: all\nits threads on one SM, together, with the same\nthread indices and the same barrier behavior. So a block\nmeans the same on a small GPU and big one.\n\nSince blocks are independent, it lets the hardware shuffle\nblocks freely. Since every CUDA GPU honors the same rules for blocks,\nmeans shuffling never changes what a block computes.\n\nPut together: same program, any GPU, same answer. The GPU\nonly decides how many blocks to run at once, never what they do.",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
